# Copied to /etc/rivora/rivorad.env (mode 0600) by install-systemd.sh.
# Self-signed HTTPS is on by default for publicly bound APIs
# (apiListen: 0.0.0.0:9870). Browsers will warn; curl needs -k.
#
# Bearer auth. REQUIRED when apiListen is not loopback: install-systemd.sh
# refuses to install without it, and the web console signs in with this token.
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 24
# RIVORA_API_KEY=
#
# Optional read-only key: may read the API and console but not drain or reweight
# backends. Needs RIVORA_API_KEY as well (rivorad refuses to start otherwise).
# Hand this one to dashboards and anyone who only needs to look.
# RIVORA_API_READONLY_KEY=
#
# Either variable accepts a comma-separated list, which is how a key is rotated
# with no outage: RIVORA_API_KEY=<new>,<old>, move clients to <new>, then drop <old>.
#
# Or use file-backed certs instead of auto self-signed:
# RIVORA_TLS_CERT=/etc/rivora/tls.crt
# RIVORA_TLS_KEY=/etc/rivora/tls.key

RIVORA_TLS_SELF_SIGNED=1

# Extra rivorad flags. -persist-datapath keeps the XDP/TCX links attached across
# rivorad restarts (no traffic gap) but also after `systemctl stop`; remove the
# datapath with `rivorad -detach`. See the runbook, "Restarting rivorad".
# RIVORAD_ARGS=-persist-datapath
